# `kk`

`kk` performs the real principal-value Kramers-Kronig transform of a tabulated
function. If $S(E)$ is the selected interpolant on $[a,b]$, the reported value
at an interior point is

$$
K(z)=\frac{1}{\pi}\operatorname{PV}\int_a^b\frac{S(E)}{E-z}\,dE.
$$

## Usage

```text
kk [options] input output
kk [options] -
```

The second form reads two-column `x y` data from standard input and writes only
numeric result rows to standard output. Diagnostics and errors are written to
standard error.

Input is line-oriented. Blank lines and full-line comments whose first
non-whitespace character is `#` are skipped. Every other physical line must
contain exactly two finite representable numbers. Inline comments, partial or
extra records, malformed fields, and values that overflow or underflow to zero
are errors reported with the source and line number.

Input is sorted by `x`. The coordinates must then be strictly increasing and
their total span must be finite. The input must contain an even number of
points on a grid symmetric around zero. The result is evaluated on that grid.

In file mode, input and output must not be the same filesystem object,
including through a symbolic or hard link. Parsing and transformation finish
before the output is opened, so an existing output survives those failures.
Output open, write, flush, and close failures are fatal. Writing is not an
atomic replacement: a failure after the destination is opened can leave it
truncated or incomplete.

## Options

| Option | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `-h`, `--help` | Print help. |
| `-i METHOD`, `--interpolation METHOD` | Select `linear`, `cspline`, `akima`, or `steffen`. Default: `akima`. |

`linear` is piecewise linear and requires at least two points. `cspline` is a
natural cubic spline and requires at least three points. `akima` is a local
piecewise-cubic interpolant and requires at least five points. `steffen` is a
monotonicity-preserving piecewise-cubic interpolant and requires at least three
points. `cspline` and `akima` can overshoot between samples. `steffen` stays
within each interval's endpoint range, but its second derivative can be
discontinuous and its shape can be conservative near extrema.

## Numerical Algorithm

The selected GSL interpolant is materialized once as a polynomial on each input
interval: degree one for `linear` and degree three for the other methods. `kk`
integrates each polynomial divided by $E-z$ analytically using polynomial
division and logarithms, then combines the interval contributions with
compensated summation. It does not use adaptive quadrature and has no
integration-tolerance, workspace, quadrature-rule, or GSL-error-policy options.
Intermediate moment calculations use wider or exact arithmetic when native
precision cannot reliably distinguish cancellation from a true zero.

At $z=a$ or $z=b$, the ordinary one-sided transform generally has a logarithmic
divergence. For compatibility, `kk` reports the finite endpoint-subtracted
value

$$
K_{\mathrm{sub}}(z)=\frac{1}{\pi}\int_a^b
\frac{S(E)-S(z)}{E-z}\,dE.
$$

The displayed `Sum` is likewise evaluated analytically from the interval
polynomials.
